.he "inetd (8)                      11/03/26                     inetd (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
inetd
- the internet superserver
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
/bin/inetd
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Inetd reads /etc/inetd.conf, opens one socket per service on the
network card (sys/NET.md), binds its port, arms the listener and
selects over the descriptors.  A service that is not being used costs
no process, which is the point: the card has one listening socket per
service whether or not a daemon waits on it.
.sp
A line is
.sp
.nf
.in +4
<port> <program> [args...]
.in -4
.fi
.sp
and '#' starts a comment.  A blank line and a line starting with #
are skipped.  The port is the config's, not the protocol's: 514 and
513 on real hardware, anything high under d1, which binds the guest's
port number on the host.
.sp
When a listener is ready the connection is accepted on a duplicate, so
the listener keeps its descriptor, and the service is forked with the
connection on standard input, output and error.  inetd appends three
arguments to the program: -i, the caller's dotted quad, and inetd's
pid.  The program takes the connection on fd 0, calls no open, bind or
accept of its own, and kills the pid when it is done.  That is how this
process reaps: the kernel has no SIGCHLD, so a finished service stops
the parent and the next pass calls wait.  A program that exits without
the kill leaves a zombie until the process table fills.
.sp
The listener is armed once with NETLISTEN, which no longer blocks: the
card keeps it armed and an ACCEPT reopens it after taking a connection.
A wake that was another socket's frame is an EAGAIN and costs no child.
.sp
The card has eight sockets, and every listener holds one for the life
of this process.  A command session on rshd holds two more (its
connection and the stderr back-channel), a login one.  Keep the file to
the services that are actually run.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/dev/net0	the card's socket clone
.br
/etc/inetd.conf	the service table
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
